	"comments": [
		{
			"type": "Line",
			"value": " An operator token after a would-be `<...>` close that is not an expression",
			"start": 20,
			"end": 97
		},
		{
			"type": "Line",
			"value": " start (acorn's `startsExpr = false`) holds the close, so the whole stays an",
			"start": 99,
			"end": 177
		},
		{
			"type": "Line",
			"value": " instantiation expression rather than a relational chain. Matches acorn.",
			"start": 179,
			"end": 253
		},
		{
			"type": "Line",
			"value": " Necessary but not sufficient: `>`, `>>`, `>>>` and `<<` carry no `startsExpr`",
			"start": 255,
			"end": 335
		},
		{
			"type": "Line",
			"value": " either, yet acorn holds the close for none of them. The first three no parser",
			"start": 337,
			"end": 417
		},
		{
			"type": "Line",
			"value": " admits — the `input_invalid_*` files beside this one; `<<` tsc reads as an",
			"start": 419,
			"end": 496
		},
		{
			"type": "Line",
			"value": " instantiation and tsv repairs, so it has no invalid file to sit in. The `>`-led",
			"start": 498,
			"end": 580
		},
		{
			"type": "Line",
			"value": " assignment operators `>>=` and `>>>=` are invalid files too: tsc takes no list",
			"start": 582,
			"end": 663
		},
		{
			"type": "Line",
			"value": " ahead of any `>`-led token, and acorn refuses the instantiation as a target. So",
			"start": 665,
			"end": 747
		},
		{
			"type": "Line",
			"value": " is every close GLUED to a `>`-led operator (`f<T>>= c`, `f<A<B>>=c`), which the",
			"start": 749,
			"end": 831
		},
		{
			"type": "Line",
			"value": " longest punctuator swallows, leaving a comparison assigned to.",
			"start": 833,
			"end": 898
		},
		{
			"type": "Line",
			"value": " `in` / `instanceof` — binary keyword operators.",
			"start": 901,
			"end": 951
		},
		{
			"type": "Line",
			"value": " Equality operators.",
			"start": 1006,
			"end": 1028
		}
	],
